<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <record model="res.groups.privilege" id="privilege_helpdesk">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Access</field>
            <field name="category_id" ref="base.module_category_operations_helpdesk"/>
        </record>

        <record id="group_helpdesk_user" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk User</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_helpdesk"/>
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.group_user'))]"/>
        </record>

        <record id="group_helpdesk_manager" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Manager</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_helpdesk"/>
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('group_helpdesk_user'))]"/>
            <field name="user_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.user_admin')), (4, ref('base.user_root'))]"/>
        </record>

        <record id="group_ai_triage_external_service" model="res.groups">
            <!-- External-AI ticket-triage allow-list, 2026-09-23. Full reasoning:
            hams_com's docs/proposals/CHILD_SAFETY_COMMUNICATIONS_CONSENT.md section G
            ("External-AI visibility into hams_helpdesk tickets is allow-listed, not
            ban-listed") and the to-do this closes,
            night_shift_todo/medium/duplicate-service-account-privilege-helpdesk-security-af9e1c30.md
            (both in hams_com).

            Why this group exists at all: user_ai_triage_service's real backend is Google's
            Gemini API (via the `agy` CLI / daemons/hams_ticket_triage_mcp in hams_com), a
            genuine external, third-party cloud AI, not a local/on-device model, and it
            used to share group_helpdesk_manager with user_helpdesk_service, giving the two
            differently-named, differently-trusted accounts identical grants (no real
            privilege separation; flagged by hams_shared/tools/check_burn_list.py). This
            group replaces that shared grant with one scoped to exactly what an
            external-AI-facing account needs: read-only access to hams_helpdesk.ticket (see
            ir.model.access.csv), gated further by rule_helpdesk_ticket_ai_triage_external_allowlist
            below on the specific ticket categories external AI is allowed to see at all.

            Why allow-list, not ban-list: a ban-list (block specific sensitive categories,
            allow everything else by default) requires remembering to add every future
            sensitive category to the ban list the moment it's created, and a category someone
            forgets to add is visible to external AI by default, the wrong failure mode for
            anything that could touch child safety. This group's own module now DOES define
            a csam_enticement_trafficking category (see helpdesk_ticket.py's own
            _CSAM_TICKET_TYPE, added by this same NCMEC-reporting-workflow branch) -- and it
            is deliberately never added to the allow-list below. The allow-list is the
            reverse of a ban-list: external AI sees NO ticket category by default, and a
            category becomes visible to it only once someone deliberately adds it here.
            Today's allow-list (rule_helpdesk_ticket_ai_triage_external_allowlist) covers the
            two ordinary categories that existed before this branch, 'general' and
            'hams_local_relay', neither of which is sensitive. Whoever later wants
            csam_enticement_trafficking visible to external AI is responsible for deciding
            that deliberately, on its own merits -- it is not anticipated or hardcoded here,
            and per the proposal's own reasoning (an external AI vendor's own abuse-detection
            systems could flag hams.com's own account as the party transmitting apparent
            CSAM), it is not expected to ever join this list.

            Why privilege_helpdesk (not a new privilege category): pager_duty/security/
            security.xml already establishes the precedent that one privilege category can
            hold several non-hierarchical, narrowly-scoped groups side by side:
            privilege_pager_duty holds group_pager_admin, group_pager_service,
            group_pager_mcp_triage_service (the direct model for this group) and
            group_pager_incident_creator, none of which imply one another. This group follows
            that same shape under privilege_helpdesk rather than introducing a whole new
            privilege category for a single group.

            Deliberately NOT implied by / implying group_helpdesk_user or
            group_helpdesk_manager: those groups' own ir.rule
            (rule_helpdesk_ticket_user / rule_helpdesk_ticket_manager) would OR together with
            this group's rule on the same model (Odoo ir.rule records for the SAME user are
            OR'ed, not AND'ed, across every group that user is in; see
            pager_check_website_company_rule's own comment in pager_duty/security/security.xml
            for the same gotcha already caught there once), which would silently defeat the
            allow-list the moment this group's user also picked up either of those. Base
            internal-user status (base.group_user) is granted directly on the service-account
            user record below instead, exactly matching how
            pager_duty.user_pager_mcp_triage_service is defined, not via implied_ids on this
            group. -->
            <field name="name">AI Ticket Triage External Service Account Group</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_helpdesk"/>
        </record>

        <record id="rule_helpdesk_ticket_user" model="ir.rule">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Ticket User</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_hams_helpdesk_ticket"/>
            <field name="domain_force">[('company_id', 'in', company_ids), '|', ('user_id', '=', user.id), ('user_id', '=', False)]</field>
            <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('group_helpdesk_user'))]"/>
        </record>

        <record id="rule_helpdesk_ticket_manager" model="ir.rule">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Ticket Manager</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_hams_helpdesk_ticket"/>
            <field name="domain_force">[('company_id', 'in', company_ids)]</field>
            <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('group_helpdesk_manager'))]"/>
        </record>

        <record id="rule_helpdesk_ticket_portal" model="ir.rule">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Ticket Portal</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_hams_helpdesk_ticket"/>
            <field name="domain_force">[('company_id', 'in', company_ids), ('partner_id', '=', user.partner_id.id), '|', ('website_id', '=', False), ('website_id', '=', user.website_id.id)]</field>
            <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('base.group_portal'))]"/>
        </record>

        <record id="rule_helpdesk_ticket_ai_triage_external_allowlist" model="ir.rule">
            <!-- The actual enforcement point for the allow-list described on
            group_ai_triage_external_service above: a group alone controls whether the
            external-AI service account can see hams_helpdesk.ticket at all (see
            ir.model.access.csv), not which records within it. This domain is what keeps
            excluded categories out of its query results even if that account's model-level
            access were ever accidentally widened some other way. Update THIS list, and only
            this list, when a category is deliberately approved for external-AI visibility;
            csam_enticement_trafficking is never added (see group_ai_triage_external_service's
            own comment above). -->
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Ticket AI Triage External Allowlist</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_hams_helpdesk_ticket"/>
            <field name="domain_force">[('company_id', 'in', company_ids), ('ticket_type', 'in', ['general', 'hams_local_relay'])]</field>
            <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('group_ai_triage_external_service'))]"/>
        </record>
    </data>
    <data>
        <!-- Helpdesk Service Account for automated tasks -->
        <record id="user_helpdesk_service" model="res.users">
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Service Account</field>
            <field name="login">helpdesk_service_internal</field>
            <field name="company_id" ref="base.main_company"/>
            <field name="company_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.main_company'))]"/>
            <field name="notification_type">email</field>
            <field name="is_service_account" eval="True"/>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="group_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('group_helpdesk_manager')])]"/>
        </record>

        <!-- AI Ticket Triage Service Account, 2026-09-22: a dedicated, single-purpose account for
        the AI-context MCP server that reads new tickets and posts an internal (admin-only, mail.mt_note)
        note with its analysis, not user_helpdesk_service, matching this codebase's own convention of
        one narrow service account per feature (see docs/proposals/GENERALIZED_AI_CONTEXT_MCP_SERVER.md,
        in hams_com).

        Revised 2026-09-23: was group_helpdesk_manager (identical to user_helpdesk_service's own
        grant, the duplicate-service-account-privilege finding check_burn_list.py flagged; see
        night_shift_todo/medium/duplicate-service-account-privilege-helpdesk-security-af9e1c30.md
        in hams_com). This account's real backend is Google's Gemini API, a genuine external/cloud
        AI, so it now gets group_ai_triage_external_service instead; see that group's own long
        comment above for the full allow-list rationale. base.group_user is listed directly here
        (not via implied_ids on the group itself) to match pager_duty.user_pager_mcp_triage_service's
        own definition exactly. A (6, 0, ...) replace, not (4, ...) add: on an upgrade of an
        existing database this actively drops the old group_helpdesk_manager membership rather
        than leaving it behind, matching user_pager_incident_creator's own comment in
        pager_duty/security/security.xml for the same reasoning. This record deliberately stays in
        this non-noupdate <data> block so that drop actually takes effect on -u. -->
        <record id="user_ai_triage_service" model="res.users">
            <field name="name">AI Ticket Triage Service Account</field>
            <field name="login">ai_triage_service_internal</field>
            <field name="company_id" ref="base.main_company"/>
            <field name="company_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.main_company'))]"/>
            <field name="notification_type">email</field>
            <field name="is_service_account" eval="True"/>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="group_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('group_ai_triage_external_service'), ref('base.group_user')])]"/>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
